154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This study examines how Italian media discourse on femicide evolved alongside the adoption of the Codice Rosso \(2019\), a law aimed at prioritizing responses to gender-based violence through faster procedures and harsher penalties. Grounded in feminist media theory and a view of discourse as both a cultural artefact and a vehicle of power, we combine computational methods to interrogate the narrative and grammatical structures through which gender-based violence is made visible, or not. Rather than assume media as neutral intermediaries, we treat them as political institutions embedded in editorial, ideological, and algorithmic economies. This approach allows us to ask whether and how language shifts in tandem with other institutional changes. What emerges is not a discursive rupture, but a reconfiguration of silencing: the Codice Rosso entered the media lexicon, yet the narrative forms that depoliticize violence largely endured. Perpetrators remain peripheral, structural critique is rare, and femicide is normalized through procedural framings.)
/Pg 7 0 R
/K [9 10]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 7 0 R
/K [5]
>>
