158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (reinforce broader gendered attitudes [9]. Femicide is rarely framed as a human rights issue or part of a pattern of structural violence [3]. Such narratives are not inconsequential: they shape how societies interpret violence and function as a crucial mechanism through which institutional change is demanded and interpreted [1] [6].)
/Pg 7 0 R
/K [11 12]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (But what happens when such change takes place? Legal reforms often emerge from sustained public attention, social movement pressure, and shifting cultural norms [11] [19]. As such, media discourse and legal change may evolve together as institutions shaped by broader societal transformations of how VaW is understood and responded to. While some studies have qualitatively examined the representation of VaW in Italian media over time [4], limited empirical research exist assessing whether femicide discourse shifted during periods marked by legal reform.)
/Pg 7 0 R
/K [13 14]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This study addresses that gap by examining how public discourse evolved alongside a moment of heightened legal attention, as part of a broader socio-symbolic landscape of legal and narrative transformations. By combining computational methods   with   a   feminist    theoretical    lens,   our)
/Pg 7 0 R
/K [15]
>>
