237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (femicides in Italy, following pressures from feminist advocacy groups [4] [9], has been noticed in the syntactic structure of news coverage. We observe a statistically significant increase in referencing perpetrators when describing the act of killing and the cause of harm. Despite the marginal rise, active phrasing remains rare, as the subject of the sentence for both categories remains the victim considerably more than the killer. In order for Italian newspapers to contribute to the eradication of VaW alongside legal reform, efforts must be made to recenter the narrative around the perpetrators, departing from their long history of victim blaming [4] [9].)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This study has limitations. Our sentiment analysis relied on a large language model \(ChatGPT\), which, despite its capacity for context-sensitive evaluation, introduces opacity and potential classification errors. While we validated its outputs through limited human cross-checking, we did not perform full-scale manual coding, which constrains the interpretability and replicability of our results. In light of the other methods, these considerations do not discard our argument that media framing is shifting toward a normalisation of femicides, failing to reflect the broader social changes that led to the introduction of the Codice Rosso.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (and emotional resonance without structural depth. Yet this tendency is likely to intensify as news production continues to adapt to social media logics and AI-driven content, where speed, visibility, and algorithmic ranking outweigh discursive nuance.  )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In an environment where headlines are often the only part of a story users read and share, we suggest future research should focus more critically on the linguistic framing of headlines themselves.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Moreover, what may appear as neutrality in media discourse invisibilizes power relations and dilutes accountability. Language is a political device [18], and this becomes evident in the continued preference for depoliticized-over-structural framings, or in the limited grammatical shifts that place responsibility on perpetrators as shown by our analysis. Therefore, media could never adopt an unpolitical stance in the face of a violence that our discussion proves to be political, as every article is a result of choices about whose actions are visible, whose suffering is narrativized, and whose power remains unspoken. We thus consider the rise of neutrality observed another way of refusing to name power.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8 9]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10 11]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (What emerges from our study is not simply the failure of journalistic language to change, but the limits of institutional reforms when they are not absorbed into public   discourse.   The   symbolic  power  of  Codice)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [12 13]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
