/T (Peer Review Response)
/E (Peer Review Response)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (We would like to sincerely thank both reviewers for their thoughtful and constructive feedback, which significantly improved the clarity, coherence, and analytical strength of our final paper. Below we outline how we responded to the comments and suggestions provided. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10 11]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Framing and Causality )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (A key suggestion from both reviewers concerned our causal framing: specifically, the need to avoid presenting the Codice Rosso as a direct cause of media change. We took this seriously and reshaped our theoretical framing. Instead of positioning media discourse as responsive to legal reform, we now frame both institutional and discursive shifts as potentially emerging from broader sociocultural transformations. We also revised our abstract accordingly to communicate this nuance upfront.)
/Pg 16 0 R
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Clarifying Contributions and Implications )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Reviewers asked a clearer articulation of the societal and      scientific      relevance     of      our    findings. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [14 15]
>>
endobj
